-- SECTION 7 — FACTUAL VERIFICATION REGISTER (Decision 244 §6)
-- ================================================================
-- "How Salmon Find Their Way Home" (text_type = 'informational') is the
-- only new passage presenting real-world factual claims. Verified this
-- session via live web search (not merely recalled), per the standing
-- FACTUAL VERIFICATION CONTROL convention (Decision 229, migration 152
-- header):
--
--   Claim: young Pacific salmon imprint on the Earth's magnetic field
--   (its inclination and intensity) at the point where they first enter
--   the sea, and use this remembered "magnetic map" to navigate the
--   open ocean back toward the correct general region on return.
--     SOURCE-CONTAINS: Journal of Experimental Biology, "A sense of
--       place: pink salmon use a magnetic map for orientation";
--       ScienceDirect, "Animal Navigation: Salmon Track Magnetic
--       Variation"; peer-reviewed research on "An Inherited Magnetic Map
--       [that] Guides Ocean Navigation in Juvenile Pacific Salmon" --
--       multiple independent, mutually-consistent sources.
--     ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: the passage describes this as remembering
--       "its particular strength and angle the way a person might
--       remember a specific address" -- an original simplification
--       avoiding technical terms (inclination/intensity) not suitable
--       for a 10-11-year-old reader, without misstating the underlying
--       mechanism.
--     FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: HIGH (multiple independent, peer-reviewed,
--       mutually-consistent sources).
--     UNRESOLVED-CONTESTED-CLAIMS: None identified.
--
--   Claim: juvenile salmon become imprinted on their home stream's
--   distinctive chemical odour before migrating to sea, and use this
--   remembered odour to identify and enter the correct stream/tributary
--   on return -- a hypothesis first proposed by Arthur Hasler and Warren
--   Wisby in 1951, since confirmed by further research.
--     SOURCE-CONTAINS: the original Hasler and Wisby olfactory-imprinting
--       hypothesis (1951); Springer chapter "Imprinting to Olfactory
--       Cues: The Basis for Home-Stream Selection by Salmon"; Hasler and
--       Scholz's dedicated 1983 monograph "Olfactory Imprinting and
--       Homing in Salmon" -- multiple independent, mutually-consistent
--       sources spanning several decades of confirming research.
--     ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: the passage names both scientists and the
--       1951 date as a single, clean retrieval fact (mirroring how the
--       bee passage names Karl von Frisch and 1946), without detailing
--       the specific experimental methods used.
--     FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: HIGH (foundational, multiply-replicated,
--       decades of confirming research).
--     UNRESOLVED-CONTESTED-CLAIMS: None identified.
--
--   Claim: the two navigation systems form a genuine staged handover --
--   the magnetic sense is necessary for long-distance open-ocean
--   navigation because olfactory cues are too diluted to detect from far
--   away, while the olfactory sense takes over for precise home-stream
--   identification once the salmon is close enough to detect it, because
--   the magnetic sense alone is not precise enough to distinguish one
--   stream from another.
--     SOURCE-CONTAINS: Alaska Department of Fish and Game, "How Salmon
--       Find Their Way"; Geophysical Institute, "Do Salmon Navigate by
--       the Earth's Magnetic Field?" -- both describe the same
--       complementary, range-dependent relationship between the two
--       systems.
--     ANGEL-SIMPLIFICATION: the passage presents this as "handing over
--       from one system to the next", an original explanatory framing;
--       it does not claim a precise, quantified distance threshold at
--       which the handover occurs, since the sources reviewed this
--       session describe the relationship qualitatively rather than
--       with an exact figure.
--     FACTUAL-CONFIDENCE: MEDIUM-HIGH (the qualitative staged-handover
--       relationship is well-supported across independent sources; the
--       precise distance/mechanism of the handover itself remains an
--       area of ongoing research, appropriately not asserted here as
--       more exact than the evidence supports).
--     UNRESOLVED-CONTESTED-CLAIMS: the exact mechanism by which a
--       salmon's brain manages the transition between the two senses is
--       still being actively researched -- the passage's own closing
--       paragraph states this explicitly ("Researchers continue to
--       study exactly how...") rather than presenting the handover as a
--       fully solved mechanism.
--
